==============================================================================
 STREAMCLIPPER (Clipper-OP)  -  START HERE
==============================================================================

 This file answers the basic questions: what is this, why use it, what do I
 install, how do I run it, how do I use it, and what do I do when it breaks.
 (The longer technical notes are in README.md. This file is the simple one.)


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1. WHAT IS THIS?
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

 StreamClipper is a tool that runs on YOUR computer. You give it a long
 video, a game stream or a podcast, and it finds the best moments (funny,
 loud, exciting) and cuts them into short vertical clips (9:16, 1080x1920)
 for Reels / Shorts / TikTok, with captions already burned in.

 - It does NOT post anything anywhere. You review the clips first.
 - Everything runs locally. The internet is used only to download the source
   video and, once, the Whisper models and the caption font during setup.
 - You are responsible for having the rights or permission to clip any
   video you add.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 2. WHY USE IT? (what problem it solves)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

 Watching a 2-hour stream just to find 5 good moments is slow. This tool:

   1. Listens to the audio and reads the speech (transcript).
   2. Scores moments using loudness, laughter/shouting, keywords, chat
      spikes and (optionally) face/motion changes.
   3. Cuts clips that start 2-4 seconds BEFORE the moment and end on a
      sentence end or a natural pause (not mid-word).
   4. Crops to vertical while tracking the face, adds big captions with the
      active word highlighted, and sets the loudness to about -14 LUFS.
   5. Flags problems (clip starts mid-sentence, silence at the start/end,
      face cut off, captions missing) and shows the reason.

 It also has a LIVE mode: it watches a live stream and makes clips a few
 seconds after something happens.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 3. WHAT YOU NEED (requirements)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

 Operating system : Windows 10/11 with PowerShell (the setup/run scripts
                    are .ps1 files)
 Python           : 3.11 (install 3.11 specifically, see note below)
 FFmpeg + FFprobe : must be a FULL build (needs libass for captions and
                    libx264). The "Gyan.FFmpeg" winget package is fine.
 Deno (optional)  : a JavaScript runtime. Helps YouTube downloads work.
 Disk space       : at least 5 GB free (downloads, caches, clips)
 RAM              : 8 GB is workable; config.toml limits memory use to 5 GB
 Internet         : for setup and for downloading source videos

 Python packages (installed automatically from requirements.txt):

   fastapi==0.115.6
   uvicorn==0.34.0
   yt-dlp[default]>=2026.08.19
   faster-whisper==1.1.1
   numpy>=1.26
   opencv-python-headless==4.10.0.84
   websockets>=12,<16
   cryptography>=42

 NOTE about Python version: setup.ps1 runs "py -3.11 -m venv .venv" when the
 Python launcher (py) exists. So if you only have Python 3.12 or 3.13, setup
 will fail with "Python 3.11+ is required". Installing Python 3.11 with the
 command below avoids that.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 4. INSTALL EVERYTHING IN ONE GO
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

 Open PowerShell (right-click Start > Terminal / Windows PowerShell).

 STEP A - Install the programs (Python, FFmpeg, Deno). Copy and paste this
 whole block once. Then CLOSE PowerShell and open a new one so the new
 programs are found:

   winget install -e --id Python.Python.3.11 ; winget install -e --id Gyan.FFmpeg ; winget install -e --id DenoLand.Deno

 STEP B - Go to the project folder (change the path to where you unzipped it):

   cd "C:\path\to\Clipper-OP-main"

 STEP C - Install all Python packages, download the caption font and the
 Whisper models, and run the health check. This is ONE command, and it is
 the same command you use to start the app every time:

   .\run.ps1

 The first run takes a while (it creates the .venv folder, installs the
 packages, downloads the Anton font and the Whisper models). After that,
 it starts in a few seconds.

 If PowerShell says "running scripts is disabled", run this once in the same
 window and then try again:

   Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope Process -ExecutionPolicy Bypass

 --- Prefer to install the Python packages manually? ---------------------
 Inside the project folder, in PowerShell:

   py -3.11 -m venv .venv
   .\.venv\Scripts\python -m pip install --upgrade pip
   .\.venv\Scripts\python -m pip install -r requirements.txt
   .\.venv\Scripts\python -m streamclipper fetch-models

 Or, as a single pip line without requirements.txt (same packages):

   .\.venv\Scripts\python -m pip install "fastapi==0.115.6" "uvicorn==0.34.0" "yt-dlp[default]>=2026.08.19" "faster-whisper==1.1.1" "numpy>=1.26" "opencv-python-headless==4.10.0.84" "websockets>=12,<16" "cryptography>=42"

 Only if you want to run the tests (adds pytest and httpx):

   .\.venv\Scripts\python -m pip install -r requirements-dev.txt


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 5. HOW TO RUN
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

 Every time you want to use it:

   cd "C:\path\to\Clipper-OP-main"
   .\run.ps1

 Your browser opens at   http://127.0.0.1:8765
 (If it does not open, type that address into your browser yourself.)

 To stop the app, go back to the PowerShell window and press Ctrl+C.

 Start without opening the browser automatically:

   .\run.ps1 --no-browser

 The address and port can be changed in config.toml under [web]. Keep the
 host as 127.0.0.1 so that only your own computer can open the UI.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 6. HOW TO USE IT (the normal workflow)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

 A) Make clips from a video or podcast
    1. Open the "Video or podcast" tab.
    2. Paste a link (YouTube etc.) or the path of a video file on your PC.
    3. Choose the language:
         auto     - English stays English, Hindi/Hinglish is written in
                    Roman letters (e.g. "mai kyu aau nahi tu ja")
         en       - English only
         hinglish - force Hindi/Hindi+English in Roman letters
       If Hinglish is detected as English, pick Hinglish yourself.
    4. Choose the speed:
         fast     - base Whisper model, 720p, quicker (default)
         quality  - small Whisper model, slower encode, better text
    5. Press "Add source", then Continue.
    6. A window asks for the CLIP LOOK: layout, gaming style, caption
       position/look/size, with a live preview. Tick "Remember this look"
       to keep it for next time.
    7. Wait. The job moves through these stages:
         download > audio > transcribe > segment > score > plan >
         frame > render > check > save
    8. Open the "My clips" tab. For each clip you can: preview, trim, edit
       captions, change the layout, download, or delete it.

 B) Make clips from a LIVE stream
    1. Open the "Live stream" tab (or in PowerShell run:
         .\.venv\Scripts\python -m streamclipper live <stream-link>  ).
    2. It keeps a rolling recording (180 s by default), watches audio
       loudness and chat activity, and cuts a clip when a moment triggers.
    3. Twitch, YouTube and Kick links are recognised automatically.
    4. Chat is optional. Without chat, audio monitoring still works.
       To use chat, set the credentials in PowerShell BEFORE starting the
       app (they only last for that PowerShell window):

         $env:YOUTUBE_API_KEY = "your-youtube-api-key"
         $env:TWITCH_CLIENT_ID = "your-twitch-client-id"
         $env:TWITCH_USER_ACCESS_TOKEN = "your-user-token"
         $env:KICK_ACCESS_TOKEN = "your-kick-token"
         $env:KICK_BROADCASTER_USER_ID = "your-kick-broadcaster-id"

       YouTube key can also be saved in the app under "Profile" (stored in
       the Windows Credential Manager). Twitch needs the user:read:chat
       scope. Kick needs a public HTTPS address for its webhook, so it does
       not work on plain localhost.

 C) Layouts you can pick
    gaming  : facecam (16:9) on top, game (16:9) below, blurred background
    normal  : the 16:9 video centred, with blur above and below
    single  : one speaker, face-centred with headroom
    two     : two speakers stacked
    corner  : rounded facecam box on top of the gameplay
    Set the default in config.toml ([framing] layout / facecam_style) or
    change it per clip in the UI.


------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 7. COMMAND LIST (command line)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

 Run any of these from the project folder as:

   .\.venv\Scripts\python -m streamclipper <command>

   doctor                    check FFmpeg, libass, captions, yt-dlp, etc.
   fetch-models              download the Whisper models (one time)
   serve                     start the web UI (what run.ps1 does)
   process <link-or-file>    add a source and process it right now
   add <link-or-file>        add a source to the queue without running it
   run [job-id]              run one queued job, or all queued jobs
   list                      list jobs and their status
   export [clip-id]          final export (crf 18, 1080x1920) of ready clips
   live <link>               monitor a live stream and clip moments
   update-ytdlp [--nightly]  update yt-dlp (fixes most YouTube errors)

 Extras: add --debug before the command to save, for every clip, a contact
 sheet of 8 frames, the chosen start/end with reasons, the layout, and the
 .ass/.srt caption files (saved in data/debug/).

 Example:

   .\.venv\Scripts\python -m streamclipper --debug process "C:\Videos\game.mp4" --language hinglish --mode fast

 There is also a separate analysis tool (FragBytez clip engine) that only
 analyses a local recording and prints JSON:

   .\.venv\Scripts\python -m clip_engine "C:\Videos\game.mp4" --compact


